WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:03.969
This is the executive
briefing for Book 05 of the

3
00:00:03.969 --> 00:00:07.737
Federal Modernization Workbook,
Devices and Endpoints.

4
00:00:07.737 --> 00:00:10.525
It's for the people who
decide how the fleet moves.

5
00:00:10.525 --> 00:00:11.950
The argument is short.

6
00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:15.891
Group Policy governed devices by
where they sat in the directory,

7
00:00:15.891 --> 00:00:18.300
and that quietly did a lot of governance.

8
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:22.313
None of it comes to the cloud, so
it has to be rebuilt on purpose.

9
00:00:22.313 --> 00:00:25.475
Hybrid join is a waypoint,
not a destination.

10
00:00:25.475 --> 00:00:28.700
And servers take their own
road, through Azure Arc.

11
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:31.937
I'll take four questions:
why, what, who and how.

12
00:00:31.937 --> 00:00:36.313
Each decision slide names the decision,
its owner and when it's needed.

13
00:00:36.313 --> 00:00:39.320
Let's start with what Group
Policy was really doing.

14
00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:46.640
For twenty years, a device's
settings came from where

15
00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.795
it sat in the tree of
organizational units (OUs).

16
00:00:49.795 --> 00:00:54.722
That position quietly did five jobs:
which settings applied, which one won,

17
00:00:54.722 --> 00:00:59.245
who could change it, how often it
refreshed, and what evidence you had.

18
00:00:59.245 --> 00:01:02.770
A cloud-joined, Intune-managed
device has no position.

19
00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:07.545
Policies are assigned flatly to groups,
overlapping settings become reported

20
00:01:07.545 --> 00:01:12.320
conflicts that may not apply at all, and
devices check in about every eight hours.

21
00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.770
Intune can deliver most of the settings.

22
00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:16.982
It can't deliver the
governance around them.

23
00:01:16.982 --> 00:01:21.057
So treat this as a governance
rebuild, not a settings migration.

24
00:01:21.057 --> 00:01:23.860
Next, why running both doesn't help.

25
00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:31.635
The tempting middle ground is to
keep the GPOs and add Intune.

26
00:01:31.635 --> 00:01:35.023
But when both touch a setting,
nobody sees who wins.

27
00:01:35.023 --> 00:01:37.085
By default Group Policy wins.

28
00:01:37.085 --> 00:01:40.915
A switch reverses that, but only
for some settings; for others,

29
00:01:40.915 --> 00:01:44.285
Microsoft warns it's a race
with no guaranteed winner.

30
00:01:44.285 --> 00:01:48.829
Microsoft's own tool can measure how much
of your Group Policy maps to Intune,

31
00:01:48.829 --> 00:01:53.373
but it doesn't close the gap, and nobody
can tell you the percentage in advance.

32
00:01:53.373 --> 00:01:57.784
Microsoft's advice is to rebuild from
today's requirements rather than copy.

33
00:01:57.784 --> 00:02:02.920
So the decision for the endpoint engineering
lead: write down every overlapping

34
00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:07.672
setting before Intune enforces it, and
give each GPO copy a retirement date.

35
00:02:07.672 --> 00:02:09.600
Next, the shortcut.

36
00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:17.113
The obvious shortcut is hybrid
join, and it's almost a switch.

37
00:02:17.113 --> 00:02:20.438
Change the sync scope, publish
one setting per forest,

38
00:02:20.438 --> 00:02:23.537
and every domain-joined
device registers itself.

39
00:02:23.537 --> 00:02:26.000
No approval, no ownership check.

40
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.175
That effortlessness is the hazard.

41
00:02:28.175 --> 00:02:31.435
Microsoft says new devices
should be cloud joined,

42
00:02:31.435 --> 00:02:35.938
and hybrid join is fine for existing
devices, but never as the end state.

43
00:02:35.938 --> 00:02:39.684
And it's sticky: moving off it
later means resetting the device,

44
00:02:39.684 --> 00:02:42.549
and it still needs a domain
controller in sight.

45
00:02:42.549 --> 00:02:45.136
So every hybrid device needs an exit:

46
00:02:45.136 --> 00:02:49.188
a target of Entra join and a
trigger, like its refresh date.

47
00:02:49.188 --> 00:02:52.530
The sponsor and CIO set
the domain-unjoin waves,

48
00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:55.701
because unjoining ends
everyone's fallback.

49
00:02:55.701 --> 00:02:58.170
Next, what the shortcut costs.

50
00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:05.714
Done as an unattended switch,
hybrid join keeps the single

51
00:03:05.714 --> 00:03:09.270
sign-on and quietly spends
the governance underneath.

52
00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:11.770
New device objects appear with no owner.

53
00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:13.145
Duplicates pile up.

54
00:03:13.145 --> 00:03:17.170
Turning on the managed-device
policy too early locks people out.

55
00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:20.108
Thousands of changes can't
be traced to a decision.

56
00:03:20.108 --> 00:03:23.383
And retired machines linger,
padding the managed count.

57
00:03:23.383 --> 00:03:27.070
Even a perfect rollout only
covers domain-joined Windows.

58
00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:33.520
Macs, phones, Linux, personal devices, kiosks
and servers all need their own answer.

59
00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:38.333
So the decision: fleet-wide changes go
wave by wave, approved by a person,

60
00:03:38.333 --> 00:03:43.145
with the access policy in report-only
mode until coverage is measured.

61
00:03:43.145 --> 00:03:47.957
The endpoint lead calls each wave;
the CISO decides the enforcement.

62
00:03:47.957 --> 00:03:50.550
Now, what a governed device needs.

63
00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:58.562
After modernization, one laptop
shows up in five or six systems,

64
00:03:58.562 --> 00:04:00.775
most of them editable by someone.

65
00:04:00.775 --> 00:04:05.450
That only works if you decide, fact
by fact, where the truth lives.

66
00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:08.651
The business facts, who owns
it, what it's for and where

67
00:04:08.651 --> 00:04:12.000
it belongs in the organization,
come from procurement.

68
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:17.002
The technical facts come from the
services: Entra ID for identity, Intune for

69
00:04:17.002 --> 00:04:21.925
management and compliance, Defender
for threats, and Azure Arc for servers.

70
00:04:21.925 --> 00:04:23.000
Then one rule.

71
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:29.287
Every derived value, a tag, a group, a
category, has to trace back to its source.

72
00:04:29.287 --> 00:04:33.801
If it can't, it's drift, and you fix
it at the source, never at the copy.

73
00:04:33.801 --> 00:04:36.890
Next, how new devices start out governed.

74
00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:46.540
For new devices, the doctrine is
Intune-first: no ungoverned device, ever.

75
00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:50.240
A new laptop is governed from
the moment it first powers on.

76
00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:53.809
It never joins the old domain,
never gets co-managed later,

77
00:04:53.809 --> 00:04:57.227
and never runs without an
organizational assignment.

78
00:04:57.227 --> 00:04:59.577
The most important change is upstream:

79
00:04:59.577 --> 00:05:02.514
procurement becomes the
first governance step.

80
00:05:02.514 --> 00:05:07.307
The device's place in the organization
is set when the purchase order goes out,

81
00:05:07.307 --> 00:05:09.703
the way HR records drive user accounts.

82
00:05:09.703 --> 00:05:11.629
If anything fails along the way,

83
00:05:11.629 --> 00:05:15.778
the device goes to quarantine
instead of quietly running degraded.

84
00:05:15.778 --> 00:05:18.211
The decision for the sponsor and CIO:

85
00:05:18.211 --> 00:05:22.265
from the next purchase order, no
new device takes the old path.

86
00:05:22.265 --> 00:05:24.520
Next, the access signal.

87
00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:31.332
Device compliance is
where devices meet access.

88
00:05:31.332 --> 00:05:34.406
Intune decides whether
a device is compliant,

89
00:05:34.406 --> 00:05:38.532
and Conditional Access, from Book
04, decides what it can reach.

90
00:05:38.532 --> 00:05:42.995
Out of the box, a device with no
compliance policy counts as compliant.

91
00:05:42.995 --> 00:05:47.945
The program flips that, so a device that
slips through every group can't pass.

92
00:05:47.945 --> 00:05:51.545
A device that stops reporting
for two weeks loses access.

93
00:05:51.545 --> 00:05:55.380
And the grace period, how long
a failing device keeps working,

94
00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:57.758
is a risk decision, not a technical one.

95
00:05:57.758 --> 00:06:02.883
On the most privileged workstations, a
security detection also costs access.

96
00:06:02.883 --> 00:06:06.420
The decision for the CISO:
make compliance fail closed,

97
00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:10.495
and set the grace period, before
Conditional Access relies on it.

98
00:06:10.495 --> 00:06:13.160
Next, devices that can't enroll.

99
00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:22.246
The book allows four carve-outs: personal
devices, Linux that Intune can't manage,

100
00:06:22.246 --> 00:06:25.560
older servers, and devices
that can't enroll at all.

101
00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:29.427
That last group, lab instruments,
factory-floor systems,

102
00:06:29.427 --> 00:06:33.210
PCs locked to a vendor's
certified image, is the hard one.

103
00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:37.447
They can't pass a compliant-device
policy, so their users are blocked.

104
00:06:37.447 --> 00:06:41.930
The program handles them in order:
put them on their own network segment,

105
00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:46.488
protect them where they connect, and
only then, if people must reach cloud

106
00:06:46.488 --> 00:06:51.047
resources, a named exclusion with an
owner and a review every ninety days.

107
00:06:51.047 --> 00:06:54.923
Because that leaves a control
unmet, it's a risk acceptance.

108
00:06:54.923 --> 00:06:59.222
So the authorizing official decides
it, before those users get access.

109
00:06:59.222 --> 00:07:01.540
Next, the existing fleet.

110
00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:09.452
The existing fleet can get to the
same governed end state three ways.

111
00:07:09.452 --> 00:07:12.428
The manual path uses the
native tools by hand.

112
00:07:12.428 --> 00:07:14.864
The governed path runs the same steps,

113
00:07:14.864 --> 00:07:19.816
but every high-impact change is previewed,
approved by a person and recorded.

114
00:07:19.816 --> 00:07:22.859
And the retrofit path adds
that governance later,

115
00:07:22.859 --> 00:07:25.828
to an estate someone
already modernized by hand.

116
00:07:25.828 --> 00:07:29.638
All three start the same way:
measure before you change anything,

117
00:07:29.638 --> 00:07:31.364
and give it at least two weeks.

118
00:07:31.364 --> 00:07:33.927
Fully automatic change is refused.

119
00:07:33.927 --> 00:07:36.703
Intune also has a
built-in second approver.

120
00:07:36.703 --> 00:07:41.285
The decision for the sponsor and CIO:
use the governed path for anything

121
00:07:41.285 --> 00:07:46.102
over a few hundred devices, or wherever
an authorizing official has to sign.

122
00:07:46.102 --> 00:07:47.660
Now, who owns it.

123
00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:54.212
Under Group Policy, whoever could edit and

124
00:07:54.212 --> 00:07:57.335
link a GPO decided for
every machine beneath it.

125
00:07:57.335 --> 00:08:01.372
Now that job is split across
several teams and three consoles.

126
00:08:01.372 --> 00:08:05.136
And Group Policy taught a generation
of administrators that being

127
00:08:05.136 --> 00:08:08.497
able to change something meant
having the right to decide it.

128
00:08:08.497 --> 00:08:12.947
That habit is how someone sets the
agency's risk posture without noticing.

129
00:08:12.947 --> 00:08:15.822
A thirty-day grace period
is a risk decision.

130
00:08:15.822 --> 00:08:20.922
So is leaving an old GPO in place,
or exempting kiosks from encryption.

131
00:08:20.922 --> 00:08:23.509
The book's rule is one decider per row.

132
00:08:23.509 --> 00:08:28.917
And exceptions split by time: a temporary
one with an end date is the CISO's call;

133
00:08:28.917 --> 00:08:33.160
a standing one goes to the
authorizing official, on a schedule.

134
00:08:33.160 --> 00:08:35.120
Next, the full map.

135
00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:44.407
The sponsor and CIO decide the path, the
scope and when devices leave the domain.

136
00:08:44.407 --> 00:08:47.745
The CISO decides the baselines,
the compliance rules,

137
00:08:47.745 --> 00:08:52.195
the update schedule, and how the help
desk verifies someone before a wipe.

138
00:08:52.195 --> 00:08:54.826
Endpoint engineering decides when an old

139
00:08:54.826 --> 00:08:57.682
setting is retired and
whether each wave goes.

140
00:08:57.682 --> 00:09:00.328
Application owners decide
for their own apps,

141
00:09:00.328 --> 00:09:02.833
because only they can
tell if it still works.

142
00:09:02.833 --> 00:09:06.207
And the authorizing official
owns standing exceptions.

143
00:09:06.207 --> 00:09:10.905
The permissions live in three separate
role systems, and two warnings stand out:

144
00:09:10.905 --> 00:09:14.820
keep the all-powerful Intune
administrator role out of daily work,

145
00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:19.732
and treat anyone who can push extensions to
a server as that server's administrator.

146
00:09:19.732 --> 00:09:24.345
The decision for the CISO: a second
approver on high-impact changes.

147
00:09:24.345 --> 00:09:25.560
Now, how.

148
00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:30.210
Now the how.

149
00:09:30.210 --> 00:09:34.810
The Group Policy sunset runs one
setting and one population at a time.

150
00:09:34.810 --> 00:09:38.435
Every setting gets asked, in
order: is it still needed?

151
00:09:38.435 --> 00:09:40.510
Is there a direct Intune equivalent?

152
00:09:40.510 --> 00:09:45.535
Does a cloud service replace the need, the
way OneDrive replaces redirected folders?

153
00:09:45.535 --> 00:09:47.010
Can a script enforce it?

154
00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:51.372
Anything that fails all four becomes
a dated exception with an owner.

155
00:09:51.372 --> 00:09:55.837
Then the rules that keep it honest:
retire before you translate, one owner

156
00:09:55.837 --> 00:10:00.598
per setting, and no GPO link switched
off until its replacement shows success.

157
00:10:00.598 --> 00:10:05.147
GPOs are disabled first and kept
ninety days as a safety net.

158
00:10:05.147 --> 00:10:08.463
The decision for the
endpoint engineering lead:

159
00:10:08.463 --> 00:10:12.773
freeze every inventoried GPO, so
new requirements go to Intune.

160
00:10:12.773 --> 00:10:14.580
Next, the timeline.

161
00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:23.317
The migration runs in seven phases,
each with an exit gate and a way back.

162
00:10:23.317 --> 00:10:28.426
On the book's planning estimates for
a large fleet: inventory and classify,

163
00:10:28.426 --> 00:10:33.283
then plan the overlap, write the
baselines, pilot on about five percent,

164
00:10:33.283 --> 00:10:36.717
cut over in four waves,
verify, and formally retire.

165
00:10:36.717 --> 00:10:38.822
That's roughly thirty-four weeks,

166
00:10:38.822 --> 00:10:42.330
plus a ninety-day hold before
the last GPOs are deleted.

167
00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:43.830
Months, not weeks.

168
00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:48.406
One sequencing rule matters under
schedule pressure: on co-managed devices,

169
00:10:48.406 --> 00:10:52.474
the antivirus settings move before
general device configuration,

170
00:10:52.474 --> 00:10:55.380
because moving configuration
drags them along.

171
00:10:55.380 --> 00:11:00.406
The decision: endpoint engineering makes
each go or no-go on measured results,

172
00:11:00.406 --> 00:11:03.705
not the calendar, and the
sponsor sets the sequence.

173
00:11:03.705 --> 00:11:05.520
Next, servers.

174
00:11:09.270 --> 00:11:13.882
Servers take a different road, because
Intune doesn't manage Windows Server.

175
00:11:13.882 --> 00:11:19.232
Azure Arc gives each server an Azure
resource, with roles, tags and policy.

176
00:11:19.232 --> 00:11:22.694
But Arc is the management
plane, not the identity plane.

177
00:11:22.694 --> 00:11:27.383
Onboarding doesn't take a server off
the domain or change how people sign in.

178
00:11:27.383 --> 00:11:32.096
A connected server is only addressable,
not governed, until three things

179
00:11:32.096 --> 00:11:36.808
are attached on day one: a configuration
baseline, patching and Defender.

180
00:11:36.808 --> 00:11:41.832
Licensing changes the bill, but only
once each server's license is attested.

181
00:11:41.832 --> 00:11:47.607
And cloud sign-in works only on Windows
Server 2025, without Conditional Access.

182
00:11:47.607 --> 00:11:51.614
The decision: each server leaves
the domain as its own decision,

183
00:11:51.614 --> 00:11:54.233
after its sign-in dependencies are known.

184
00:11:54.233 --> 00:11:56.060
Next, the dates.

185
00:11:59.810 --> 00:12:06.135
Windows Server 2012's extended
updates ended on October 13, 2026.

186
00:12:06.135 --> 00:12:11.282
Any of those hosts still running gets
no more security fixes from anywhere,

187
00:12:11.282 --> 00:12:15.848
so the book treats them as a red
finding: migrate, upgrade or isolate.

188
00:12:15.848 --> 00:12:21.360
Windows Server 2016 reaches end of support
on January 12, 2027, so any you can't

189
00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:27.222
upgrade in time should be enrolled for
extended updates through Arc before then.

190
00:12:27.222 --> 00:12:32.769
Windows 11 version 23H2 stops getting
updates on November 10, and Windows 10 is

191
00:12:32.769 --> 00:12:38.060
already out of support, so each one left
is an exception with an upgrade date.

192
00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:42.924
The decision can't wait: server
infrastructure acts on the 2012 hosts now,

193
00:12:42.924 --> 00:12:46.735
and the authorizing official
accepts any that must stay.

194
00:12:46.735 --> 00:12:48.400
Next, patching.

195
00:12:52.150 --> 00:12:56.488
Once Intune owns the fleet,
update rings set the patch clock.

196
00:12:56.488 --> 00:12:59.940
Each month's update flows
through four rings: a lab,

197
00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:03.813
a small pilot, about a quarter
of the fleet, then everyone.

198
00:13:03.813 --> 00:13:08.876
With the book's values, an update reaches
every online device in about sixteen days,

199
00:13:08.876 --> 00:13:12.450
and a bad one can be paused
before it reaches the broad ring.

200
00:13:12.450 --> 00:13:15.516
Windows Autopatch can
run the rings for you,

201
00:13:15.516 --> 00:13:20.625
and it's included for GCC customers
with the government G3 or G5 licenses.

202
00:13:20.625 --> 00:13:24.987
Why assessors care: the federal patching
control asks for updates within

203
00:13:24.987 --> 00:13:29.063
a period the agency defines, and
these ring deadlines are that period.

204
00:13:29.063 --> 00:13:34.349
So the decision for the CISO: approve the
ring values as the agency's patch window.

205
00:13:34.349 --> 00:13:36.370
Next, running the fleet.

206
00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:43.623
In the run state, three
records replace the old tree:

207
00:13:43.623 --> 00:13:48.545
the policy library, what each device
reports, and the log of every change.

208
00:13:48.545 --> 00:13:51.282
Running the fleet means
keeping them in agreement.

209
00:13:51.282 --> 00:13:55.105
Proving it to an assessor means
handing over those same records,

210
00:13:55.105 --> 00:13:59.720
not a document written for the audit,
including the versioned policy library,

211
00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:02.533
per-setting status, a
weekly reconciliation of

212
00:14:02.533 --> 00:14:05.345
the device inventories,
and the update results.

213
00:14:05.345 --> 00:14:10.275
Retiring a device runs in order: save
the encryption keys first, then wipe,

214
00:14:10.275 --> 00:14:15.370
and delete the cloud device record last,
since deleting it deletes the keys.

215
00:14:15.370 --> 00:14:20.383
The decision for the CISO: own the drift
findings and evidence from the first wave.

216
00:14:20.383 --> 00:14:22.010
Let's pull it together.

217
00:14:25.760 --> 00:14:27.335
So, the decisions.

218
00:14:27.335 --> 00:14:32.340
The sponsor and CIO set the shape: an
exit plan for every hybrid-joined device,

219
00:14:32.340 --> 00:14:36.948
no new device on the old path from
the next purchase order, the governed

220
00:14:36.948 --> 00:14:41.635
path for large estates, and each server's
domain exit as its own decision.

221
00:14:41.635 --> 00:14:46.155
Endpoint engineering freezes
the GPOs, records every overlap,

222
00:14:46.155 --> 00:14:49.023
and calls each wave on measured results.

223
00:14:49.023 --> 00:14:54.069
The CISO makes compliance fail closed,
adds a second approver to high-impact

224
00:14:54.069 --> 00:14:59.510
changes, sets the patch window, owns the
evidence, and decides temporary exceptions.

225
00:14:59.510 --> 00:15:04.466
And the authorizing official accepts
the rest: devices that can't enroll,

226
